pub fn title -> String = "Grammar reference"
pub fn summary -> String =
  "Seseragi's syntax is written here using EBNF, a notation for grammars. The articles for each construct explain how that syntax behaves."
pub fn readingTitle -> String = "How to read this grammar"
pub fn reading -> String =
  "An EBNF production names a syntactic form. Quoted text is a symbol or keyword written directly in source. Unquoted names refer to other productions. A comma joins parts in order, and a vertical bar gives alternatives. Square brackets make a part optional; braces allow zero or more repetitions. The final semicolon separates EBNF productions. It does not mean every Seseragi construct needs a semicolon."
pub fn lexical -> String =
  "INTEGER and LOWER_IDENTIFIER name categories identified during lexical analysis, such as integer literals and identifiers beginning with a lowercase letter. Do not copy INTEGER into source as an identifier. The corresponding articles explain Unicode identifiers, comments, literals, and escapes. NEWLINE and source semicolons separate declarations or block items; indentation depth does not delimit blocks."
pub fn grammarTitle -> String = "Complete grammar"
pub fn grammarBody -> String =
  "This grammar is read from the language specification during the site build. It brings together the syntax for modules, declarations, expressions, patterns, and types."
pub fn limitsTitle -> String =
  "Valid syntax can still have a type error"
pub fn limits -> String =
  "Code can match the grammar and still fail because it uses an undefined name or passes a value of the wrong type. Function application associates to the left; function types associate to the right. Each operator's fixity rules determine its precedence. Conditions such as covering every case in match or making a declaration public cannot be determined from the syntax alone. Read the corresponding article for its meaning and restrictions."
pub fn ambiguity -> String =
  "For example, {} is an empty record, while { expression } is a block. A single-field shorthand record needs a comma, as in { x, }. Parentheses group an expression, form a tuple, or denote Unit. To pass multiple function arguments, separate them with spaces, as in f x y."
pub fn relatedTitle -> String = "Read the rules behind the productions"
pub fn sourceTitle -> String = "Normative source"
pub fn source -> String =
  "These productions come from the language specification's grammar. Rules in the specification also determine conditions that syntax alone does not settle, including name resolution, type checking, and exhaustive matching."
